speechtext:
That, in the opinion of this House, the government should consider the advisability of introducing a measure to amend the Constitution Acts 18671982, in order to reform the Senate by providing, in particular:
1.	that, 50 per cent of the number of Senators in each province be appointed by the Governor in Council and that the respective appointees also be residents of the province for which they are appointed;
2.	that, 50 per cent of the number of Senators in each province be appointed by the Lieutenant-Governors in Council;
3.	that, the tenure of Senate appointments be for the duration of a Parliament;
4.	that, all Senators may be re-appointed;
Senate Reform
5.	that, when a provincial election is called, the seats of all Senators of that province appointed by the Lieutenant-Governor in Council be vacated and that the said Senators may be re-appointed by the Lieutenant-Governor in Council after the provincial election;
6.	that, where a Bill is not adopted by the Senate within ten sitting days after its receipt from the House of Commons, the Senate may require that the Bill be submitted to a joint conference committee of the two Chambers;
7.	that, where a Bill is not adopted by the House of Commons within fifteen
days after its receipt from the Senate, the House of Commons may require that the Bill be submitted to a joint conference committee of the two Chambers;	.
8.	that, each House appoint seven members to the joint conference committee, and that the representation of the House of Commons reflect the proportion of the number of seats of each political party in that Chamber;
9.	that, where a conference committee proposal fails to be adopted by both Houses, the original bill be withdrawn;
and that this House urge the Senate and the Legislative Assemblies of all provinces to pass similar resolutions.
He said: I am delighted to have the opportunity to deal with this motion with you in the chair, Madam Speaker. Two other Members of this House, the Hon. Member for Edmonton South (Mr. Edwards) and the Hon. Member for Bow River (Mr. Taylor), have dealt with Private Members' Motions on the Senate. A number of my other colleagues in the House during those discussions have made comments and have spoken on the subject, as have many provincial legislatures and, indeed, the public. There has been a great deal written and a great number of comments regarding the cause of Senate reform. The thrust of most of the recommendations has focused on the so-called "triple E" concept that the Senate should be made effective, equal and elected. I want to endorse most strongly the call for effectiveness and for equality, particularly on a provincial basis. However, as you will see in a moment, Madam Speaker, I want to deal with a Senate which would be appointed.
The motion I put forward departs only from the comments made by colleagues in that they ask for an elected Senate. I propose an appointed Senate. If the only purpose of a geographically equal Senate were to balance this country's regional and population-based interest, I would probably go along with the idea of an elected Senate. However, my concept of the Senate goes beyond providing that particular balance to add the function of providing a Chamber of sober second thought, which was the initial intent. I feel that appointing the Senators will remove them from the passions and partisanship which is inevitable in the running for election.
I want to deal with the motion I put forward in some particular detail. First, I made the point that 50 per cent of the Senate should be appointed by the Governor-in-Council. I left out of my motion details such as how much land appointees should own in their particular area. I have said they should be residents of the province for which they are appointed. I purposely left this vague, because in a motion such as this, obviously, the Government would have some ideas. However, I think it is abundantly clear that if we were to appoint in the present manner only half of the Senate, the other half to be appointed by another group, we would find that those persons appointed to the Senate would tend to be legislators first. I say

March 19, 1987
Senate Reform
this without rancor and certainly without envy. Elaving been a very successful bagman for a political Party, having been the former president of a political Party, having been an organizer or in fact being an organizer for a political Party, would not necessarily be sufficient background to be named a Senator. The fact is that in the Senate I perceive, and which I support, there would necessarily be legislators first, and the Governor in Council, I think we would find as we go through this, would need to appoint men and women whose function would be legislative in the first instance.
The second point I make is that the other half of the Senate would be appointed by the Lieutenant-Governors of each province. Once again, I have purposely left the numbers vague. I think having a minimum number, for example, in Prince Edward Island, New Brunswick and those province which now have minimums, is something which can be dealt with by negotiation and by the Government of the day in consultation with the other provinces. In countries I could name, the representatives in the upper House do not vary in number that greatly despite the variables in the provinces or regions of the country which send them to the Senate. The United States, for example, has the same number of Senators in Rhode Island as it does in California. In Germany there are two Members of the upper House from the smallest of its states and only five from the largest. So some balance could be arrived at which would be fair.
In the concept I put before you, Madam Speaker, the Senators might well include a Cabinet Minister without portfolio in a province, a former Premier and very likely someone who was very closely involved with the politics of that province because he or she would be in the Upper House of Canada to deal with their regional responsibilities and they would be performing a specific duty.
The third item has to deal with both Houses and that is that the tenure of Senate appointments would be for the duration of a Parliament. There is no question that the Senators could be reappointed by Parties of either stripe. In the case of some appointments, I am sure the Governments of the provinces, or the Government of Canada, in appointing persons, might want to take from areas of business expertise certain members to put in the Senate. If they did, provision would have to be made, in my concept of the Senate, to allow for an extended leave of absence. It would be hardly fair for a corporate counsel of a major firm to be put in the Senate for one term and then expected to go back on the street. The likelihood would be that some form of leave of absence would be necessary and, as I indicated, all Senators could be reappointed.
Fifth, when a provincial election is called, the seats of all the Senators from the province in which an election is called would be vacated. The new government would appoint the Senators from that province. Rightly or wrongly, whether one agrees with the politics of the Parties I speak of, we have had in the Parliament of Canada, in the upper House, since-
